WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.687 --> 00:00:02.770
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Google Cloud Drawing

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:05.220
Board, where we doodle
our way through the cloud.

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.875
Today's topic,
what is Cloud SQL?

00:00:07.875 --> 00:00:11.950
Cloud SQL is a fully managed
relational database for MySQL,

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.380
PostgreSQL, and SQL Server.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.740
It reduces maintenance
cost and automates

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:20.040
database provisioning,
storage capacity management,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:21.960
replication, and backups.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.930
It offers quick setup, with
standard connection drivers

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.740
and built-in migration tools.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:28.530
Cloud SQL is easy to setup.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.450
You select the
region and zone where

00:00:30.450 --> 00:00:33.600
you would like the instance to
be, and it gets created there.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.180
You also have a lot of
configuration options,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.770
where you can select
the machine type

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.250
with the right number of CPU
and amount of memory you need.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.160
Choose storage each type between
solid state and hard disk

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:47.460
drives, depending on latency
queries per second and cost

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.570
requirements.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.310
And set storage capacity.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.390
Higher storage capacity
leads to better performance.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:57.690
Cloud SQL also offers automated
backups and recovery options.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.050
You can set time slots
and locations for backups.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:02.700
For production
applications, it is

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.090
recommended to enable
high availability, or HA.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.670
By enabling this feature,
the database instance

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.600
will automatically failover to
another zone in your selected

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.700
region in case of an outage.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.310
You can also create
cross-regional replicas

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.920
to protect from
regional failures.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.220
In addition, you can enable
automatic storage increase

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.250
to add more storage
when nearing capacity.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.460
Now, how can you migrate
an existing MySQL database

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.870
to Cloud SQL?

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:34.620
Cloud console makes it very
easy by providing a migrate data

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.200
button, which guides
you through easy steps.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.460
First, you provide your
data source details,

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.030
things like public IP
address, port number,

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:43.800
and your replication
credentials.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.680
Second, you create a
Cloud SQL read replica,

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:48.930
just like we discussed
in the creation process,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.010
using a SQL dump file.

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:53.730
Third, you sync the read
replica with source.

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:55.710
And, finally, you
promote the read replica

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:59.580
to primary instance
with very low downtime.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.770
Is data safe in Cloud SQL?

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.020
Well, just like anything
else at Google Cloud,

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:05.940
the data in Cloud
SQL is encrypted

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:07.770
at rest and in transit.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.660
External connections
can be encrypted

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:12.090
using SSL or Cloud
SQL Proxy, which

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.210
is a tool to help you connect
to your Cloud SQL instance

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.980
from your local machines.

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:19.590
You can use Cloud SQL
as a relational database

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.230
for your applications that are
hosted within Google Cloud,

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.390
like App Engine, Cloud Run,
Compute Engine, Kubernetes

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.080
Engine, or Cloud Functions.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.760
You can also connect
your Cloud SQL database

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.030
with applications that are
hosted outside of Google Cloud.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.290
Link for more details
is included below.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:39.420
Cloud SQL pricing varies
depending on MySQL, PostgreSQL,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:40.590
or SQL Server.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.600
Broadly speaking, though,
it's the combination

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.590
of the type of instance,
storage, or network you use.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:48.630
SQL Server also has
some licensing costs.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.930
Now, what are some of
the Cloud SQL use cases?

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.460
Since Cloud SQL is a
relational database,

00:02:54.460 --> 00:02:57.210
you can use it with any
online transaction processing

00:02:57.210 --> 00:03:00.090
apps, such as order or
payment processing apps,

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:02.280
where you need to
handle frequent queries

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.320
with fast response times.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.100
Want to learn more
about Cloud SQL?

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.500
Check out cloud.google.com/sql.

